export const OPENERS_SMALL_A = {
  "small-animals/abyssinian-guinea-pig": [
    "An Abyssinian guinea pig is Cavia porcellus, the same domestic cavy as any smooth-coated pet, with the coat grown into rosettes that radiate from center points and stand up like cowlicks. This card lists a medium adult, 1.5 to 2.5 pounds (0.7 to 1.2 kg), a lifespan of 5 to 7 years, and an origin of South America. The rosette pattern is a hair arrangement on that species, not a separate metabolism. Care level reads beginner to intermediate, and the coat is the part that adds work. Each swirl faces outward and holds hay chaff, bedding fibers, and pellet dust that a short smooth coat lets fall.",
    "Activity is listed as high, and the social need is marked very high, so a single Abyssinian is the wrong head count. Floor space on the card is a minimum of 7.5 square feet for one cavy and 10.5 square feet for two. The two-animal figure is the one that matches how they should live. A second guinea pig belongs in the plan from the start. They cannot make vitamin C. The liver of Cavia porcellus does not synthesize it, and a cowlick coat does not add that pathway. Feed the same herbivore ration other cavies need: grass hay available all day, a measured serving of fortified pellets, and vegetables that actually supply the vitamin.",
    "Rosettes collect debris. A cowlick packed with hay can hide a scab or a bald spot, so the coat check is a hand through each swirl, not a glance from the top of the cage. Teeth and gut still depend on that hay. A cavy that drops its appetite is in trouble quickly, because the species is built to keep fiber moving and it cannot fast the way a dog can. Five to seven years is the span printed here, and vitamin C has to be in the food on every one of those days. A water bottle and a hide do not stand in for the vitamin or for a companion.",
  ],

  "small-animals/african-pygmy-hedgehog": [
    "The African pygmy hedgehog is Atelerix albiventris, a small spiny mammal from Central Africa, not a miniature porcupine and not a rodent that happens to own quills. This card lists adults at 6 to 8 inches, a lifespan of 4 to 6 years, a shy and nocturnal temperament, and intermediate difficulty. The animal sleeps through the day and runs, eats, and makes a mess after the household has gone quiet. Someone who wants a pet that is awake for dinner and a weekend afternoon is buying the wrong shift.",
    "They need a warm room, about 72 to 80 F. A cooler spare bedroom can drop body temperature and leave the hedgehog sluggish and difficult to warm safely. Heat lamps aimed at a plastic bin are a burn risk, not a substitute for a room that already sits in that band. The setup is a solid-floor enclosure with a hide, a wheel, and bedding that stays dry. It is not a pocket that travels in a coat all day. This is not a low-work pocket pet.",
    "Ownership is illegal in some US states and in some cities. The keeper has to check the law where they live before anyone picks an animal or pays a breeder. A seller who ships across state lines does not write the ordinance in the buyer's town. If the answer from animal control or the state wildlife office is no, the project stops there.",
    "Anointing is normal. A hedgehog will foam saliva and spread it along the spines, often after a new smell, and the episode looks like a fit to someone who has never seen it. The behavior passes. It is not rabies, and it is not the neurologic disease described below. Waking the animal out of a deep daytime sleep to show guests will produce a ball of spines long before it produces a friendly lap pet.",
    "Wobbly hedgehog syndrome is a real progressive neurologic disease. Weakness, wobbling, and falling that do not resolve are a veterinary problem, not a quirk of a shy animal and not a repeat of anointing. The disease worsens over time. No home trick reverses it. A hedgehog that suddenly cannot stand deserves a clinician who has seen the species, and the 4 to 6 year lifespan is short enough that waiting a month uses up a large share of it.",
    "Food is an insectivore ration: a diet made for hedgehogs, plus insects, not a bowl of dog kibble and fruit. Obesity is common when the wheel is missing and the treats are constant. Teeth still matter on a small mouth, and a hedgehog that stops eating needs a scale and a vet, because a 6 to 8 inch animal has little reserve. Annual food and bedding on this card is listed in a wide band, which is a hint that the real cost tracks how the animal is actually fed and housed, not a single bag from a mall shop.",
    "Handling is a slow night project. They ball, hiss, and puff when startled, and quills are a defense, not a costume. Four to six years of cage cleaning, nail checks, temperature watching, and a legal address is the commitment behind an animal that looks easy because it is small. Intermediate on the card means the husbandry has rules. Cute photos taken at noon do not show the noise, the running wheel, or the local law.",
  ],

  "small-animals/american-rabbit": [
    "The American rabbit on this page is a domestic breed, blue or white, not a wild cottontail pulled from a yard. Cottontails are a different kind of rabbit, with different parasites, a different stress response, and no business in a pet hutch. This card lists a large adult, 9 to 12 pounds, a lifespan of 8 to 12 years, moderate activity, and a beginner care level. Eight to twelve years is a long stretch next to the giant breeds whose cards run shorter, so the pen and the hay budget have to last.",
    "Mandibular teeth grow continuously. The lower cheek teeth and the incisors do not stop at adulthood, and a mouth with nothing hard to grind wears into points, abscesses, and a rabbit that cannot chew. Grass hay is the feed that does that grinding. Pellets and vegetables sit beside the hay. They do not replace it. A diet of mostly pellets and carrots is how a 9 to 12 pound rabbit arrives at the clinic with a wet chin and a jaw that hurts.",
    "The space line is a minimum of 4 by 2 feet. That is a floor, not a carrying case, and a large rabbit needs room to stretch out flat, which is how rabbits rest when they feel safe. A wire floor cuts hocks. Bedding or a solid surface under the body belongs in the pen from the first day. Social needs are listed as pairs or groups. A companion rabbit is a second animal to bond carefully, not a guinea pig dropped in as company and not a cottontail from outdoors.",
    "Activity is moderate, which does not mean the rabbit can live in a small indoor cage between weekends. They need a daily chance to move, dig at a box, and chew safe wood. Beginner on the card assumes the hay, the floor space, and a vet who will see a rabbit. It does not assume the animal can be fed like a cat. Blue and white are the coat colors of this breed. A brown wild rabbit is not an American that has not been brushed.",
    "Watch the appetite. A rabbit that refuses hay, sits hunched, or produces no droppings is not being stubborn. The gut slows when they stop eating, and a large body still crashes. Eight to twelve years of unlimited hay, nail trims, and a pen they can lie flat in is the ordinary work. The breed is a domestic Oryctolagus, built for a pen and a show table, and it still has to chew every day of that life.",
  ],

  "small-animals/angora-rabbit": [
    "An Angora rabbit is a wool breed of domestic rabbit. It is not a guinea pig, not a long-haired cavy, and not a sheep that can be turned out to graze. This card lists a wide size, 5 to 12 pounds, because more than one wool breed shares the job, a lifespan of 7 to 12 years, a gentle and docile temperament, and intermediate to advanced difficulty. Origin is Turkey. The wool is the reason the difficulty sits that high. A calm rabbit can still die of a coat that nobody is combing.",
    "The coat needs grooming every day. Skip a week and the wool mats against the skin, pulls, and hides sores. Mats are not a cosmetic problem that can wait for a weekend. They tighten, collect urine, and make the rabbit miserable. Scissors near thin skin are easy to misuse. A keeper who does not want a daily grooming session should not take a wool rabbit home and plan to clip it twice a year.",
    "Wool block from swallowed hair is a real emergency. Angoras ingest fiber while they groom themselves, and that hair can stall the gut. A rabbit that stops eating needs a vet the same day. Waiting to see if it is picky is how a wool rabbit is presented already cold, hunched, and empty of droppings. Hay still has to be the main feed, both to keep the gut moving and to give the teeth something to wear against. Pellets alone do not do either job.",
    "Gentle and docile describes many of these rabbits with people. It does not describe the coat. Intermediate to advanced means the grooming and the gut risk, not a difficult personality that needs a harsher hand. They still need a pen, a litter area, and time out of the cage. Seven to twelve years of daily wool work is the calendar. A child who likes the look of a cloud with ears is not the grooming plan.",
    "The 5 to 12 pound span is a group of body sizes, not a promise that a single animal will swing across that range. A smaller wool rabbit and a giant angora type do not eat the same number of pellets, and neither of them should be fed like a guinea pig that gets a vitamin C tablet and a handful of hay. Rabbits make their own vitamin C. They do not make a coat that stays open without a comb. Mix those two species up and both animals lose.",
    "Shearing or a careful clip is sometimes how a badly matted rabbit gets relief, and it belongs with someone who will not cut the skin. It is not a substitute for the other fifty weeks. After a clip the wool grows back and the daily work returns. Check under the chin, the belly, and the rear, where stains and mats start. A clean face in a photograph can hide a pelt that is already felted underneath.",
    "Plan the veterinary relationship before the wool is pretty. A clinic that sees rabbits, and that understands a rabbit in gut slowdown cannot wait until Monday, is part of keeping a wool breed. Seven to twelve years, origin Turkey on this card, and a size anywhere in that 5 to 12 pound band all sit on top of the same rule: the coat is a job, the hay is the diet, and a silent, fasting rabbit is a same-day patient.",
  ],

  "small-animals/baldwin-guinea-pig": [
    "A Baldwin guinea pig is a hairless cavy, still a guinea pig in every system that matters, with bare skin instead of a coat to buffer the room. This card lists a medium adult, 1.5 to 2.5 pounds, a lifespan of 5 to 7 years, moderate activity, intermediate care, and a minimum of 7.5 square feet. Social needs are a pair. Hairless does not mean low maintenance, and it does not mean the animal can be housed like a reptile on sand.",
    "They need warmth. A cool floor, a drafty hutch, or a room that a coated cavy tolerates will chill bare skin. They also need skin care. The skin can burn and it can dry out. Direct sun through a window, a heat lamp too close to the body, and rough dusty bedding are the usual ways that happens. Cracks, flakes, and red patches are a husbandry problem to fix and, if the skin breaks open, a vet visit. Do not smear random human lotions on a cavy and call it skincare.",
    "The diet is still hay, pellets, vegetables, and vitamin C. Hairless cavies cannot make vitamin C either. The missing coat does not change the liver. Unlimited grass hay is the bulk of the food, fortified pellets cover a measured share, and vegetables have to carry vitamin C every day. A Baldwin that is fed like a hairless rat, on seeds and scraps, will fail the same way any other guinea pig fails when the vitamin is missing: rough coat is not the clue, because there is no coat. Look at appetite, joints, and energy instead.",
    "Keep them with another guinea pig. The card says pairs, and 7.5 square feet is the minimum printed for the enclosure, which gets tight once two bodies and a hide are in it. Give them more floor if the room allows. Moderate activity still means they move, popcorn, and need space to turn around without standing on each other. A solitary Baldwin in a small tub is not warmer in any way that matters. It is only more bored, and boredom does not protect skin.",
    "Bedding should be soft and clean. Urine scald shows up fast on skin with no hair to wick it, so the wet spots have to leave the cage daily. Five to seven years of that routine, plus vitamin C and a warm room, is the real care level behind the intermediate label. They are guinea pigs that you can see in detail. The skin is not a fashion choice that cancels hay, a companion, or the vitamin they cannot synthesize.",
  ],

  "small-animals/californian-rabbit": [
    "A Californian rabbit is a large domestic rabbit with a white body and dark points, bred as a commercial meat rabbit and also kept as a pet. It is not a California kingsnake, not a reptile, and not a wild cottontail from the state of California. This card lists 8 to 11 pounds, a lifespan of 5 to 10 years, moderate activity, beginner care, and a minimum pen of 4 by 2 feet. The points sit on the nose, ears, feet, and tail. The rest of the coat stays white if the rabbit is actually this breed.",
    "Large means the pen and the hay bill, not a bigger version of a dwarf that can live in a cage sold for a guinea pig. The 4 by 2 foot line is a minimum floor. A meat-breed body needs room to lie stretched out, and a wire grid under an 8 to 11 pound rabbit cuts the hocks. Solid flooring or deep bedding is part of the setup. Social needs are pairs or groups. Bond a rabbit to a rabbit. Do not add a snake, a guinea pig, or a cat and call it companionship.",
    "Hay, pellets, and vegetables are the diet line, and hay is the part that has to be unlimited. The teeth never stop growing. A Californian on a pellet-heavy ration gets fat and still manages to grow molar spurs, which is a poor trade. Leafy greens can join the hay. A bowl of carrots and yogurt drops cannot. Five to ten years is a wide lifespan band. The short end of it arrives early when the diet is sweet and the floor is wire.",
    "Beginner on the card is about a sturdy domestic rabbit with ordinary rabbit rules, not about an animal that tolerates neglect. They are moderate in activity and still need time out of the pen to move. Meat-breed history means a full body and a fast juvenile growth rate. Feed a growing Californian enough hay and a sensible pellet portion. Do not free-feed a rich mix until it cannot groom its own rear.",
    "The name collision with the kingsnake is worth killing off once. A white rabbit with dark points does not need a heat lamp, a hide box at 85 F, or a diet of mice. It needs hay, floor space, and a vet who treats rabbits. If the animal in the photo is scaled and limbless, it is on the wrong page. Five to ten years of a large rabbit is the commitment this card is actually describing.",
  ],

  "small-animals/chinchilla-mutations": [
    "Chinchilla color mutations are color varieties of Chinchilla lanigera, not new species. Ebony, beige, violet, mosaic, and white are coats on the same animal as a standard gray. This card lists a medium body, 1 to 2 pounds, a lifespan of 10 to 20 years, high activity, intermediate care, and a multi-level cage. A white or a mosaic is not a different pet with different organs, a different heat tolerance, or a different right to a dust bath. Buying a rare color does not buy a simpler chinchilla.",
    "Care matches the standard animal. They get a dust bath, not a water bath. The coat is too dense to wash and dry. Water left in the fur chills the body and ruins the texture people bought the color for. Offer dust on a regular schedule and take the bath out afterward so it is not used as a litter box. Teeth and gut need hay. Pellets made for chinchillas and limited treats sit beside unlimited grass hay. A raisin habit is not enrichment. It is how a 1 to 2 pound herbivore gets a gut ache.",
    "They are heat sensitive above about 75 F. A room that feels pleasant to a person in summer can kill a chinchilla of any color, white coat or gray. Air conditioning, a cool stone, and a plan for power cuts matter more than the shade of the fur. Do not mist them to cool them. Moisture plus heat is worse than heat alone. Ten to twenty years is long enough that several summers have to be survivable, not just the week they come home.",
    "The card lists high activity and says they can live in pairs. They are crepuscular, busy at dusk and dawn, and they will also be up at night. A pair has to be introduced, not dumped together because the color chart looked nice. Two animals means more floor, more hides, and a cage with height. The multi-level line on this card is the minimum shape. A single short hamster cage cannot hold a chinchilla that jumps, and a color mutation does not jump any lower.",
    "Intermediate care is the dust, the heat, the hay, and the decade-plus life, shared by every color on this page. Violet, beige, ebony, mosaic, and white all need the same pellets, the same avoidance of water baths, and the same refusal of a hot room. If a seller describes a white chinchilla as a separate species with easier care, the seller is wrong about both the taxonomy and the husbandry. The lifespan line remains 10 to 20 years either way.",
  ],

  "small-animals/chinchilla-standard-gray": [
    "The standard gray chinchilla is the wild-type color of Chinchilla lanigera, the dense gray coat the species is known for, not a lesser version of a mutation and not a different animal from the ebony or beige. This card lists a medium adult, 1 to 2 pounds, a lifespan of 10 to 20 years, high activity, intermediate care, and a multi-level cage. They can live well over 10 years. The card's span already says so. Plan for the long end, not for a hamster-sized commitment that happens to look soft.",
    "The fur is dense enough that a water bath is the wrong tool. They clean in dust. Offer a dust bath several times a week, then remove it so the pan stays a bath and not a toilet. Water soaked into that coat does not flash off the way it does on a short-haired dog. It sits, chills the animal, and mats the texture. A gray that looks dull may need better dust and less humidity in the room, not a rinse in the sink.",
    "Heat kills them. Rooms held above about 75 F are already dangerous, and a hotter afternoon is an emergency for the animal even when the people in the house are only uncomfortable. Standard grays have no special resistance because the color is natural. Cool air, shade from direct sun at the window, and a ride-out plan for a broken air conditioner belong in the setup. Do not cool a chinchilla with water.",
    "They are crepuscular. The busy hours are dusk and dawn, with plenty of movement at night, which is what the card's high activity looks like in a real room. A multi-level cage with solid shelves gives them somewhere to jump. Wire floors and plastic ledges they can chew through are a poor match for a 10 to 20 year life. Hay has to be available all the time for the teeth and the gut. Pellets are a side dish. Treats stay limited. Pairs can work when the animals accept each other. A lone chinchilla still needs the height, the dust, and the cool room.",
    "Intermediate is an honest label for an animal that dies of a hot week and lives long enough to need a decade of hay. One to two pounds is a small body with a long memory and a lot of hair. The gray coat is the wild type. It still cannot be washed, still cannot sit in a warm apartment in July, and still has to grind hay so the molars stay in bounds. Ten to twenty years is the lifespan to budget, and well past ten is a normal reading of that line, not a record.",
  ],

  "small-animals/chinese-hamster": [
    "The Chinese hamster is Cricetulus griseus, a small hamster from northern China and Mongolia with a longer tail than a dwarf hamster. It is not a Campbell's, not a winter white, and not a Syrian that stayed small. This card lists 3 to 5 inches (8 to 13 cm), a lifespan of 2.5 to 3 years, high activity that is crepuscular and nocturnal, and intermediate care. The body is slim. The tail is visible. Someone expecting a round Phodopus with a stub has the wrong species in the box.",
    "They are often kept solitary, and the card says solitary is preferred, the way it is for Syrians. Do not buy two because a dwarf-hamster page said pairs are possible. That advice belongs to a different genus. Floor space is a minimum of 450 square inches, which is a footprint, not a tiny travel cage with a wheel crammed on top. They need a solid wheel that will not catch that longer tail, hides, and bedding deep enough to dig. A wire wheel and a bare tray are how tails and feet get hurt.",
    "The diet is omnivore: seeds, vegetables, and some protein, offered as a hamster mix they can actually eat, not a bowl of sunflower seeds alone. Two and a half to three years is a short life, and it gets shorter if the cage is small and the animal is paired with a stranger that fights. Intermediate care is the species difference. They look like a pet-shop hamster and then nip, or fade, when they are kept on dwarf or Syrian rules. Check the tail and the scientific name before you set the housing.",
  ],

  "small-animals/continental-giant": [
    "A Continental giant is a very large domestic rabbit of the UK type, doing a similar job to a Flemish giant and not a wild hare. Hares have longer legs, a different build, and no place in a pet pen. This card lists a very large adult, 12 to 25 pounds, a lifespan of 4 to 7 years, moderate activity, intermediate care, and a very large enclosure. Use those figures. Do not borrow the Flemish weight range or the Flemish lifespan and paste them onto this breed. The top of this card is heavier, and the years printed here are 4 to 7.",
    "The pen has to match a body that can reach 25 pounds. A very large enclosure means the rabbit can lie flat, turn, and take a few hops, not a hutch sized for a 4 pound Dutch. Moderate activity does not shrink the animal. A giant that sits all day still occupies a giant's floor when it stretches. Nail care matters more as the weight climbs. Overgrown claws change how a heavy rabbit stands and add pressure to the hocks. Trim them before they curl, and keep the flooring solid so the feet are not standing on wire.",
    "Hay, pellets, and vegetables are the diet, with hay as the bulk. A 12 to 25 pound rabbit eats a serious amount of grass hay, and the teeth still grow without it. Pellets are a measured portion. A free-choice pellet bin plus no hay produces a heavy rabbit with a sore mouth. Social needs are listed as social. Add another rabbit only with a real introduction, and do not pair a giant with a dwarf that can be injured in a scuffle over a bowl.",
    "Intermediate care is mostly size. Lifting a rabbit at the top of that weight range takes a person who can support the chest and the hindquarters and set the animal down without a drop. A fall from chest height can break a back. Children should not be the handlers of a Continental giant. Four to seven years is the lifespan on this card. It is shorter than several smaller breeds on this site, which is a reason to take the orthopedic and diet work seriously instead of treating the rabbit as indestructible because it is big.",
    "They are domestic rabbits, UK giant type, with the same need to chew and the same gut that slows when appetite stops. A giant that refuses hay needs a vet quickly. The enclosure, the nail routine, and the hay storage are the setup. A hare from a field is not a cheaper substitute and will not settle into this job. Twelve to twenty-five pounds and 4 to 7 years are the numbers to plan around.",
  ],

  "small-animals/coronet-guinea-pig": [
    "A Coronet guinea pig is a long-haired cavy with a single rosette crest on the forehead. The crest is one neat rosette over the eyes, and the rest of the long coat falls back over the body. Printed figures here are a medium adult of 1.5 to 2.5 pounds, a life of 5 to 7 years, moderate activity, intermediate care, and at least 7.5 square feet of floor. They are meant to live as a pair. The hair is the reason care sits at intermediate. The species underneath is still a guinea pig.",
    "Both the crest and the rear coat need grooming. The forehead rosette mats into a knot if it is ignored, and the long hair at the back picks up hay, bedding, and damp bedding from the floor. A daily comb of those two zones prevents the kind of felt that has to be cut off later. Bathing a guinea pig to fix mats is a poor idea. They chill easily, and a wet long coat stays wet. Clip only when a mat is already tight, and keep scissors off the skin.",
    "Vitamin C is not optional. Coronets cannot synthesize it any more than a short-haired cavy can. Hay, pellets, and vegetables are the diet line, and the vegetables plus fortified pellets are where the vitamin has to come from every day. Hay is still unlimited, because the teeth and the gut did not change when the coat got long. A silky rear end is not a sign that the diet can shift toward fruit and treats.",
    "Keep them in pairs, with at least the 7.5 square feet this card names, and more if two long coats are sharing a hide. Moderate activity means they still run and popcorn. A pen full of furniture and no open floor makes a long-haired cavy both bored and dirtier, because the coat drags through every wet corner. Choose a companion that will not chew the crest into a stump. Watch introductions. Long hair can hide a nip until the skin under it is already angry.",
    "Intermediate is the grooming plus the ordinary cavy rules, held for 5 to 7 years. A Coronet that misses vitamin C will not announce it with a rough coat you can see through the hair. Watch appetite, walking, and weight instead. The single forehead crest is the breed marker. It is also a daily tangle. Rear coat, hay, a pair, and vitamin C are the rest of the page, and none of them is canceled by a show-style haircut the week before a photo.",
  ],

  "small-animals/dumbo-rat": [
    "A dumbo rat is a fancy rat, Rattus norvegicus, with ears set low and wide on the sides of the head. It is not a smaller species, not a dwarf rat, and not a mouse with large ears. This card lists a small to medium adult, 0.5 to 1.5 pounds, a lifespan of 2 to 3 years, high activity, beginner care, and a minimum of 2 cubic feet per rat. That weight range is the ordinary pet-rat range. A dumbo that stays well under it is not a teacup variety. It is a young rat or a thin one.",
    "Care matches other pet rats. The ear placement does not change the lungs or the mammary tissue. Respiratory disease is common in the species, and so are tumors, especially mammary tumors. A new lump, a wheeze, red staining around the nose, or weight loss needs a vet who will treat rats. Pet-shop drops are not a plan, and this page will not invent a dose. The low ears still have to be checked for tears and for mites, but they are not a different organ system.",
    "They are highly social. The card calls for pairs or groups. Females often do better in pairs, settling with another female more readily than a pair of adult males that were raised apart. Males still need company. A lone dumbo is not a low-drama choice. It is a rat missing the species it was built to sleep beside. Introductions belong on neutral ground. Dropping a new adult into a lived-in cage is how a beginner-labeled pet draws blood.",
    "Feed lab blocks as the base and add fresh food beside them. A seed mix as the whole diet lets a rat pick the fatty pieces and ignore the rest. Two cubic feet per rat is the printed minimum, and a pair needs that allowance twice, plus height for hammocks and a solid floor. Wire mesh under the feet causes bumblefoot. High activity means climbing, chewing, and a cage that is redecorated often. Beginner care assumes you can meet that, not that the animal occupies a corner like a fish.",
    "Two to three years is an honest lifespan. A dumbo that reaches four is old, not evidence that the card was cautious. Use the short clock to take lumps and noisy breathing seriously in year two instead of waiting for a problem to declare itself. The ears are the visible trait. The medicine, the need for a partner, and the 0.5 to 1.5 pound body are the same as any other fancy rat sharing the cage row.",
  ],

  "small-animals/dutch-rabbit": [
    "The Dutch rabbit is a domestic breed marked with a white belt and colored cheeks, not a nationality of wild rabbit and not any rabbit that happens to live in the Netherlands. A brown cottontail does not become Dutch because of geography. This card lists a small to medium adult, 3.5 to 5.5 pounds, a lifespan of 5 to 8 years, moderate activity, beginner care, and a minimum of 4 by 2 feet. The belt, the cheeks, and the colored hindquarters are the pattern. A rabbit without that white saddle is a different breed or a mix.",
    "Hay is the diet that matters. Pellets and vegetables are on the card beside it, and they stay in supporting roles. The teeth keep growing for the whole 5 to 8 years. A Dutch rabbit that lives on pellets and sweet mixes still weighs only a few pounds, so a molar spur or a gut slowdown shows up fast. Unlimited grass hay, a small pellet portion, and greens the rabbit actually eats are the ordinary bowl. Treats are not a personality.",
    "Space is the 4 by 2 foot minimum, which is a floor a 3.5 to 5.5 pound rabbit can lie flat on. Smaller is not acceptable because the breed is called small-medium. They still stretch, hop, and need a solid surface. Wire floors hurt a light rabbit as surely as a heavy one. The card wants them kept as pairs or in a small group. Bond rabbits to rabbits. A guinea pig is the wrong roommate: different language, different diet, and a body that loses a kick.",
    "Activity is moderate and the care level is beginner only when the hay and the floor are already in place. They need time outside the pen in a rabbit-proofed room. Cords, baseboards, and houseplants are all on the menu if the room is not ready. Colored cheeks and a white belt make them easy to photograph and easy to mistake for a hardy toy. They are a small domestic rabbit with a full set of rabbit rules.",
    "Five to eight years of nail trims, hay, and a companion is the span to take seriously. A Dutch rabbit that stops eating is a same-day vet case, the same as any other rabbit, because the gut does not pause politely. The breed is the belt and the cheeks. It is not a wild European rabbit with a passport, and it is not a dwarf. Feed the teeth, give the 4 by 2 foot floor, and keep the pattern talk separate from the husbandry.",
  ],

  "small-animals/dwarf-hamster": [
    "This page covers two dwarf hamsters, Campbell's (Phodopus campbelli) and the winter white (Phodopus sungorus). It does not cover Syrians, and it does not cover Roborovskis. Both animals here are small and nocturnal. The card lists 3 to 4 inches (7 to 10 cm), a lifespan of 1.5 to 2.5 years, high activity that is also crepuscular, intermediate care, and a minimum of 450 square inches of floor. Origin is Central Asia and Siberia. A Syrian is a larger, solitary hamster in a different genus. A Roborovski is the third Phodopus and is not the pair described here.",
    "Campbell's are often more aggressive in groups. The card says these dwarf hamsters can sometimes live in same-sex pairs, which is a maybe, not a promise, and it fails more often with Campbell's. Fighting looks like chasing that does not stop, bitten ears, and one animal camping on the wheel while the other stops eating. Separate them. Do not wait for a bond to appear after blood. Winter whites can change coat color with day length, fading toward white when the days are short and darkening again when the light lasts longer. That molt is normal. It is not a sign you bought a Campbell's that is ill, and it is not a reason to add a sun lamp.",
    "Both species need the 450 square inches, a solid wheel, and an omnivore diet of seeds, vegetables, and some protein. One and a half to two and a half years is the whole life, so a hamster that is already adult in the shop has spent a visible piece of it. They are awake at night and around dawn and dusk. A child who wants a noon pet will mostly see a pile of bedding. Intermediate care is telling the two species apart, refusing to house them like Syrians or like Roborovskis, and treating a pair as a trial that can end in two cages.",
  ],

  "small-animals/dwarf-hotot": [
    "A dwarf Hotot is a small white rabbit with dark bands around the eyes. It is not a full-size Blanc de Hotot, which carries a similar eyeliner mark on a much larger body. Mixing the names puts the wrong adult weight in the plan. This card lists a small adult, 2.5 to 3.5 pounds, a lifespan of 7 to 10 years, moderate activity, beginner care, and a minimum of 4 by 2 feet. Seven to ten years is a long time for a rabbit that looks like it could live in a carrier.",
    "Hay, pellets, and vegetables are the diet, and hay is the staple. A 2.5 to 3.5 pound rabbit has the same continuously growing teeth as a giant. The mouth is smaller, so spurs and a dropped appetite show up on a body with less reserve. Keep grass hay in front of them all day. Measure the pellets. A dwarf that free-feeds a rich mix becomes a round rabbit that cannot reach its own cecotropes, and those missed cecotropes are food, not trash.",
    "The floor should be solid, not wire. A light rabbit still gets sore hocks on a grid, and the 4 by 2 foot minimum is the floor size, not a suggestion that a wire breeding cage from a shed is close enough. Bedding or a resting mat belongs where they sit. Company should be another rabbit or a small group, which is what this card asks for. Match them with rabbits of a size that will not accidentally injure a 3 pound animal, and introduce them properly. A Blanc de Hotot is the wrong roommate if the point of the pairing is two dwarfs.",
    "Moderate activity and a beginner label still mean daily exercise outside the pen. Rabbit-proof the room. A dwarf Hotot will chew the same cords a larger rabbit chews. The eye bands are skin and fur pigment, not a medical problem and not goggles you can wipe off. If the eye itself is wet, red, or closed, that is a vet visit, separate from the breed mark. Do not buy colored drops from a pet aisle and aim them at the band.",
    "Seven to ten years of hay, a solid 4 by 2 foot floor, and nail trims is the keeping of this breed. They are small domestic rabbits with a specific white coat and dark eye bands. They are not the standard Hotot scaled down in care requirements, and they are not a cage-only ornament. Appetite is the health check. A dwarf Hotot that stops eating needs a rabbit vet the same day, because 3 pounds does not leave much time.",
  ],

  "small-animals/emperor-scorpion": [
    "The emperor scorpion is Pandinus imperator, a large West African scorpion. This card lists 5 to 8 inches, a lifespan of 5 to 8 years, a docile and slow-moving temperament, and an origin of West Africa. The size includes the body and the pedipalps people notice first. Slow and docile describes a healthy adult that would rather threat-posture than chase. It does not describe a plastic toy, and it does not cancel the sting or the paperwork.",
    "They are listed on CITES Appendix II. International movement is regulated, and the animals in the pet trade should be captive-bred with documents a keeper can actually trace. That sourcing rule, together with the humidity, is why this is not a beginner invertebrate even though the card's difficulty line is rosy and the venom is not in the deathstalker class. If a seller cannot say where the scorpion was bred, do not take it home and sort the legality out later.",
    "They want humidity and a hide. A dry desert kit with a sand-colored background is the wrong habitat. The substrate needs to hold moisture, the hide needs to sit tight against the body, and the ventilation has to exist without turning the enclosure into a crisping box. A scorpion that stays on the water dish all day is often telling you the rest of the cage is too dry. Five to eight years is a long time to guess at that balance.",
    "They can be kept in small groups of similar size. Similar is the condition. A much larger animal will treat a smaller cagemate as food, and a freshly molted scorpion is soft and easy to damage. Give more than one hide so every animal can get out of sight. Do not crowd a group because the species is sometimes communal. Feed enough that they are not hunting each other between meals. Insects, not leftover table scraps, are the prey.",
    "The venom is mild compared with a deathstalker. Mild is a comparison, not a claim that the sting is imaginary. A healthy adult can still puncture skin, and a person who swells, vomits, or has trouble breathing after a sting needs medical care. Do not keep this animal on a shoulder or in a pocket to prove the venom point. Forceps, a calm hand, and a plan that keeps guests from tapping the lid are the handling policy. There is no dose on this page because none belongs here.",
    "Ultraviolet light makes them fluoresce. The cuticle glows under a UV source, which is a way to confirm where a dark animal is sitting, not a vitamin the scorpion has to bask under. They do not need a reptile UVB lamp the way a desert lizard does. Leaving a powerful lamp on the enclosure to make the glow permanent overheats a species that wanted a humid hide, not a spotlight. Use UV as a check, then turn it off.",
    "Five to eight inches and 5 to 8 years, from West Africa, with a slow walk and a mild sting, still adds up to a regulated animal in a humid box. Buy captive-bred. Keep the paperwork. Hold the humidity. House only similar sizes together. The beginner label some charts paste on emperors skips the two parts that actually remove people from the hobby: a dry cage, and an animal that should not have been sold.",
  ],

  "small-animals/english-angora": [
    "The English Angora is the wool rabbit with furnishings on the face and the ears, a coated head rather than the cleaner face of a French Angora. This card lists a medium adult, 5 to 7.5 pounds, a lifespan of 7 to 12 years, low activity, advanced care, and a minimum of 4 by 2 feet. Social needs are social. The furnishings are the breed point and the husbandry problem. Wool grows into the eyes, the ear edges, and the cheeks, and it mats there faster than a keeper expects from a body that only weighs about 5 to 7.5 pounds.",
    "Grooming is heavier than on a French Angora. The French coat is a job. The English coat adds the head. Miss the face and the eye weeps under a curtain of fiber. Miss the ears and the tips felt into hard plugs. A full comb-through on a schedule, not a pat on the top of the cloud, is the advanced label on this card. Low activity means they will not exercise a mat out of the coat. They will sit, eat, and grow more wool on top of the tangle.",
    "Wool block is the risk that makes the grooming medical. Swallowed hair stalls the gut. Hay comes first in the diet for that reason and for the teeth. Pellets and vegetables are on the card and stay secondary to grass hay available all day. A wool rabbit that refuses hay, grinds its teeth, or produces no droppings needs a veterinarian before the day is over. Do not wait for the coat to look worse. The coat can look enormous while the gut has already stopped.",
    "The 4 by 2 foot minimum is a floor, and it should be kept clean because furnishings drag through urine and feces. A dirty rear felted into the wool is painful and is also more fiber headed for the stomach when the rabbit grooms. Solid flooring protects the hocks. Social housing can work, and a companion will not comb the face. Seven to twelve years of head-to-toe wool is the calendar behind advanced care. Shearing a matted rabbit is a rescue step. The wool returns.",
    "Low activity is not a license to shrink the pen or to skip exercise. They still need movement to keep the gut and the weight in line, especially on a diet that has to stay hay-first so the fiber intake is high. Five to 7.5 pounds is the size on this card. Do not feed an English Angora like the heavier wool breeds and do not feed it like a guinea pig. The face furnishings identify the breed. Hay, a clean floor, and a grooming habit that includes the ears identify a keeper who can finish the 7 to 12 years.",
  ],

  "small-animals/english-lop": [
    "The English lop is the lop with very long ears, a large domestic rabbit whose ears are the breed and the injury risk. It is not a small dwarf lop, not a Holland lop, and not a mini lop that stayed a little leggy. This card lists a large adult, 9 to 11 pounds, a lifespan of 5 to 7 years, moderate activity, intermediate care, and a minimum of 4 by 2 feet. The ears can trail and spread in ways a compact lop never will. Housing that ignores that spread is how the ears get hurt.",
    "Ears injure easily. They are thin-skinned, easy to cut on a rough edge, and slow to heal when a crate door shuts on them. Check the pen for gaps at floor level, exposed wire ends, and any lid that drops. A rabbit this size will shake its head and slap an ear into a wall. Blood along the ear margin is a vet visit, not a stain to watch for a week. Five to seven years is short enough that a chronic ear wound uses up a real fraction of the life.",
    "The ears can be stepped on. That happens when people, children, or another animal move through a tight pen while the lop is resting with the ears flung out. It also happens when the rabbit is on the floor of a busy room. The 4 by 2 foot minimum on this card is already modest for a 9 to 11 pound body with ear leather spread beside it. Give more floor if you can. Do not stack furniture in the only open space and leave the ears nowhere to lie.",
    "Ear canals need checking. The canal under a heavy lop ear stays warm and poorly aired, and wax, odor, or head shaking means the ear needs a look, not a cotton swab driven downward. This page does not include a drug dose. A rabbit vet can see the canal. A keeper's job is to notice the head tilt, the scratch, and the smell early. Long ears are not a funnel you clean like a water bottle.",
    "Diet is hay, pellets, and vegetables, with hay unlimited so the teeth and the gut keep up. Social needs are pairs. Introduce a partner carefully. A scuffle that would bruise a short-eared rabbit can tear an English lop ear. Moderate activity means they need exercise that does not turn into a chase across a room full of chair legs. Intermediate care is the ears plus the ordinary large-rabbit work, held for the 5 to 7 years on the card.",
    "Handle the whole rabbit, not the ears. Those ears are not handles and not leashes. Support the 9 to 11 pound body and let the ear leather follow. A dwarf lop's care sheet will not cover the length, the stepping risk, or the canal trouble this breed actually has. Solid flooring, hay, a pair if you can bond one, and a pen where the ears can lie flat without meeting a shoe are the setup that matches this card.",
  ],

  "small-animals/fennec-fox": [
    "The fennec fox is Vulpes zerda, a small desert fox from North Africa. It is not a dog, not a fennec-shaped terrier, and not a pet that will settle because someone calls it a pup. This card lists 9 to 16 inches, a lifespan of 10 to 14 years, a playful, energetic, and independent temperament, and advanced difficulty. Origin is North Africa. The ears are large. The body on the card is still a fox body, with fox teeth, fox smell, and fox hours.",
    "The first step is the law where the person lives. Many US states and many cities ban or restrict fennec foxes. A breeder's website, a cute import photo, and a neighbor who owns one are not permission. Look up the state rule and the city or county rule before you look at prices or enclosures. This page will not describe how to evade a ban, how to hide an animal, or how to relabel a fox as something else. If the law says no, the project ends.",
    "They are nocturnal. The playful burst happens when a dog would be asleep. Expect activity late, vocal noise at night, and an animal that does not want a noon walk as its main outing. Independent on the card is accurate. They are not biddable in the way a herding dog is, and a training plan copied from a puppy class will not turn a fennec into an off-leash companion. Ten to fourteen years is a long time to be wrong about that.",
    "They dig. A pen that would hold a dog will not hold a fennec if the floor is soil and the edges are hopeful. They need a large secure enclosure with a dig barrier, a roof or a top that cannot be climbed out of, and locks a small nose cannot solve. An apartment crate is not that enclosure. Advanced difficulty is largely this engineering plus the legal question, not a trick of diet.",
    "They scream. The contact call is loud, high, and easy to mistake for a human in trouble, and it carries at night. Apartment walls and close neighbors are part of the decision. They also mark. Urine is a territory tool, including on belongings, and it does not stop because the fox is fond of someone. You can clean it. You should not expect it to vanish because the animal is small.",
    "Food is a fox diet, not a bowl of dog kibble as the whole plan and not a cat's free-choice bin. Insects, meat, and a prepared ration suited to a small canid are the shape of it. Do not feed a fennec as if the ears made it a different digestive system from other foxes. A vet who will see a fox has to be found before you need one at midnight. Dog clinics can refuse the appointment. Ten to fourteen years of that relationship is part of the cost the card's difficulty line is pointing at.",
    "Playful and energetic describes a healthy fennec that has room to run and something to dig. It does not describe a lap pet that happens to be exotic. Nine to sixteen inches, origin North Africa, lifespan 10 to 14 years, and a legal address come first. The enclosure has to stay closed. The local law has to say yes. If either of those is missing, do not buy the fox and try to retrofit permission.",
  ],

  "small-animals/ferret-albino": [
    "An albino ferret is Mustela putorius furo with a white coat and red eyes. It is the same species as a sable ferret. Color is not a breed with different medicine, and the red eyes do not come with a second set of organs. This card lists a medium adult, 1.5 to 4 pounds, a lifespan of 6 to 10 years, very high activity, intermediate care, and a multi-level cage plus play time. Social needs favor pairs. The albino is a pigment version of the ordinary domestic ferret.",
    "The extra risk that does track with the color is sunburn, not a unique liver or a unique pancreas. Pale skin and red eyes burn under direct sun and under strong window light. Shade, a cage out of the noon glare, and a limit on outdoor time protect the skin. That is coat and pigment. Insulinoma and adrenal disease, which show up as ferrets age, occur in albinos for the same reasons they occur in dark ferrets. Do not accept a claim that a white ferret is exempt, or that it needs a different drug family, because the eyes are pink.",
    "As they age, insulinoma and adrenal disease become the illnesses to watch. Weight loss, hind-end weakness, gum that looks pale, a swollen vulva, or hair loss on the tail and body are reasons to see a ferret vet, not to switch treats and wait a season. This page does not list doses. Six to ten years means many albinos will be middle-aged in the middle of that span, which is when those diseases are found. A clinic that only sees dogs may miss the pattern.",
    "They need a tall, secure cage. Multi-level on this card means ramps and shelves a 1.5 to 4 pound body can actually use, with bar spacing a ferret cannot squeeze through. They also need several hours out of the cage every day. Very high activity in a locked single level becomes screaming, chewing, and a ferret that sleeps in its food. They cannot sweat heat away. A hot room, a car, or a cage in a sunroom can overheat an albino faster than a keeper expects, and the white coat does not reflect that problem away.",
    "Some places ban ferrets, including California, Hawaii, and New York City. Check the state and the city before you acquire a pair. Pairs are preferred on this card. A single albino still needs the hours out and the tall cage. Feed a high-protein ferret food, not a cat food chosen at random and not a sweet raisin paste. Six to ten years of a heat-sensitive, legally restricted, highly active animal is the commitment. The red eyes change the sunburn risk. They do not rewrite the species.",
  ],

  "small-animals/ferret-sable": [
    "A sable ferret is Mustela putorius furo in the dark coat: a mask across the face and dark legs, with a warmer body color between them. Sable is not a separate species from the albino and not a bloodline with its own diseases. This card lists the same medium size, 1.5 to 4 pounds, the same lifespan of 6 to 10 years, very high activity, intermediate care, and a multi-level cage plus play time. If a shop says the mask means a hardier organ system, the shop is selling a color.",
    "The cage has to be tall and escape-resistant. Ferrets compress through gaps that look too small for the head, and a sable in the walls of a house is a sadder problem than a missing hamster. Several hours out, every day, match the very high activity line. A playpen or a blocked room with cords lifted and recliners checked underneath is the usual way those hours happen. They will sleep in a hammock for a long stretch and then spend the next stretch opening every cabinet.",
    "Sweat is not available as a cooling system, and dark fur on the mask and legs does not add one that a white ferret lacks. Hot cars, direct sun, and stuffy rooms are dangerous for both. Offer shade and a cool room in summer, and do not leave a sable to nap against a window. Hydration and a chance to lie on a cool floor matter more than the shade of the guard hairs. Overheating looks like a flat, panting animal. That animal needs out of the heat immediately and a vet if it does not come around.",
    "Adrenal disease and insulinoma are the age-related diseases of this species, sable coat included. Hair loss that starts at the tail, itchiness, a swollen vulva on a spayed female, sudden weakness, or drooling can be those diseases. They become common as ferrets move through the 6 to 10 year span. A dark mask does not delay them. See a clinician who treats ferrets, and do not dose a dog product from the cabinet because the symptoms sound vaguely hormonal. No dose belongs on a care page.",
    "Feed a high-protein ferret diet. Pairs are what the card prefers, and two sables need two sets of hours out plus a cage both can use without one hoarding the only hammock. California, Hawaii, and New York City are among the places that ban ferrets. The mask does not create an exemption. Confirm the rule where you live, then set up the tall cage. One and a half to four pounds, six to ten years, dark face and dark legs: that is a domestic ferret in a common color, with the full medical and legal list attached.",
  ],

  "small-animals/flemish-giant": [
    "The Flemish giant is one of the largest pet rabbit breeds, a domestic Oryctolagus cuniculus with an origin of Belgium in the 16th century. This card lists a giant adult, 9 to 14 pounds and up (4 to 6.4 kg and up), a lifespan of 5 to 8 years, low to moderate activity, and intermediate care because of the size. The enclosure minimum is 5 feet by 2 feet. That floor is the starting point, not a crate on a balcony. A small apartment hutch is the wrong offer. This breed is not a starter rabbit for a tight indoor cage.",
    "They need floor space, hay, and a handler who can lift a heavy rabbit without dropping it. Support the chest and the hind end together. A 14 pound rabbit that kicks free and falls can break its back, and a person who cannot take the weight should not be the one picking it up. Children are not that handler. Hay is the diet, with vegetables and a measured pellet portion beside it. The herbivore gut and the teeth still depend on grass hay even when the activity line says low to moderate. A giant that lies around still has to chew.",
    "The card describes them as docile and people-oriented, which is a fair account of many Flemish giants and a bad reason to skip the pen. Five to eight years of a large body means nail trims, a solid floor under heavy hocks, and a hay bill that looks like a horse's when you buy by the month. The Belgium origin and the 16th century date explain the breed's age as a livestock rabbit. They do not shrink the adult. Nine to fourteen pounds and up, in a pen at least 5 by 2 feet, with someone strong enough to lift them, is the keeping this page is about.",
  ],

  "small-animals/flying-squirrel": [
    "The animal on this card is the sugar glider, Petaurus breviceps, a marsupial. The heading says sugar glider and flying squirrel together, and those are not the same pet. A North American flying squirrel in the genus Glaucomys is a rodent. A sugar glider is not a rodent, not a squirrel, and not a mix of the two. This card lists a very small adult, 2 to 5 ounces, a lifespan of 10 to 15 years, high nocturnal activity, advanced care, and a tall cage with branches. Use the glider. Do not stock the cage as if a Glaucomys had arrived.",
    "They are colony animals. Highly social on the card means a glider kept alone is the welfare problem, not a simpler version of the pet. The usual plan is two or more compatible gliders. A single animal plus a person who is asleep all night is not a colony. They bond to their own group, sleep in a pouch together, and call when the group is split. Ten to fifteen years is a long night shift to spend correcting a one-glider purchase.",
    "The diet is sap, nectar, and protein. The card's line, a specialized diet plus fruits and insects, fits under that only when fruit is a part and not the whole meal. Insects and a prepared glider ration carry the protein. Nectar and sap substitutes carry the carbohydrate they are built to use. A bowl of fruit every night is how calcium falls behind and a glider starts to weaken in the hind legs. Two to five ounces leaves very little margin for a menu made of mango.",
    "They are illegal or permit-only in some US states, including California, Alaska, Hawaii, and others. A breeder who will ship is not the permitting agency. Read the rule where you live before the tall cage is purchased. This page will not explain how to get around a ban. If the state or the city says no, the glider stays out of the house. Advanced care assumes the animal is legal to possess and that a vet who has seen a marsupial is available. A rodent clinic checklist will miss the pouch, the diet, and the colony.",
    "Nocturnal and arboreal is the shape of the housing. A tall cage with branches, not a hamster tub, gives them height to climb and a place to glide short distances. The membrane along the body is skin, not a costume, and it tears if it catches on a wire toy. Expect barking and crabbing at night. Expect scent. Ten to fifteen years, 2 to 5 ounces, a colony, and a sap-nectar-protein diet are the card and the species together. Anyone who wanted a North American flying squirrel needs a different animal, a different license conversation, and a different page.",
  ],
};
